    'Grappling hook' => 'Grappling hook',
    'Grappling hook description' => 'Their rigid hooks were typically made of either two, three, or four welded together cast iron bars. Though hooks that could be folded flat were known to exist, they tended to cost roughly twice as much as a standard hook.',
    'Greataxe' => 'Greataxe',
    'Greataxe description' => 'A greataxe is a military two-handed melee weapon in the axe weapon group. A greataxe has high crits, dealing an extra 1d12 damage at levels 1-10, 2d12 damage at levels 11-20, or 3d12 damage at level 21-30, on a critical hit.',
    'Greatclub' => 'Greatclub',
    'Greatclub description' => 'Greatclubs were bigger and heavier than regular clubs and typically augmented with spikes, nails and/or bands of iron. Greatclubs were typically considered martial weapons due to their extra weight and spikes making it a bit more difficult to wield without hurting ones self.',
    'Greatsword' => 'Greatsword',
    'Greatsword description' => 'A derivative of the longsword, the greatsword was the largest member of the sword family, requiring two hands to wield it. The hilt was elongated to accommodate this extra grip and the blade was doubled-edged. Some versions had parrying hooks (Parierhaken) on their blade, about 4-8 in (0.1-0.2 m) away from the guard. The blades were typically dull between their parrying hooks and guard so that they could be gripped there.',
    'Halberd' => 'Halberd',
    'Halberd description' => 'The business end of a halberd consisted of three parts: the axe-head, the back spike or "fluke", and the spear tip that surmounted it. While many polearms had a similar structure, halberds had a few distinguishing features. Their axe-heads were concave (curving inward) to nearly straight, instead of convex like most axes. And were set at an angle to the shaft, instead of being mounted perpendicular.',
    'Half Plate' => 'Half Plate',
    'Half Plate description' => 'Half plate consists of shaped metal plates that cover most of the wearers body, it does not include leg protection beyond simple greaves that are attached with leather straps',
    'Hammer' => 'Hammer',
    'Hammer description' => 'Hammers whose use was intended as tools included sledgehammers, peg- and tone-setting mallets, spike and chisel hammers, hammers with claws for nail removal, and smithing hammers. Smithing hammers, in particular, consisted of solid and heavy flat-nosed steel heads with triangular tails to allow for detailed work, attached to a long duskwood handle that provided effective thermal isolation.',
    'Hammer, sledge' => 'Hammer, sledge',
    'Hammer, sledge description' => 'Sledges were large, heavy, well-balanced hammers.',
    'Handaxe' => 'Handaxe',
    'Handaxe description' => 'Handaxes were similar to hatchets in size and shape. Their shafts were usually made of wood, though metal was sometimes used. Their blades were single-edged and their back sides were either unadorned, spiked, or had a peen. Handaxes were heavier than throwing axes and not balanced enough to be used effectively as a thrown weapon.',
    'Healers kit' => 'Healers kit',
    'Healers kit description' => 'This kit is a leather pouch containing bandages, salves, and splints. The kit has ten uses. As an action, you can expend one use of the kit to stabilize a creature that has 0 hit points, without needing to make a Wisdom (Medicine) check.',
    'Herbalism kit' => 'Herbalism kit',
    'Herbalism kit description' => 'This kit contains a variety of instruments such as clippers, mortar and pestle, and pouches and vials used by herbalists to create remedies and potions. Proficiency with this kit lets you add your proficiency bonus to any ability checks you make to identify or apply herbs. Also, proficiency with this kit is required to create antitoxin and potions of healing.',
    'Hide' => 'Hide',
    'Hide description' => 'This crude armor consists of thick furs and pelts. It is commonly worn by barbarian tribes, evil humanoids, and other folk who lack access to the tools and materials needed to create better armor',
    'Holy water (flask)' => 'Holy water (flask)',
    'Holy water (flask) description' => 'As an action, you can splash the contents of this flask onto a creature within 5 feet of you or throw it up to 20 feet, shattering it on impact. In either case, make a ranged attack against a target creature, treating the holy water as an improvised weapon. If the target is a fiend or undead, it takes 2d6 radiant damage. A cleric or paladin may create holy water by performing a special ritual. The ritual takes 1 hour to perform, uses 25 gp worth of powdered silver, and requires the caster to expend a 1st-level spell slot.',
    'Horn' => 'Horn',
    'Horn description' => 'Horns were a type of wind musical instrument. Several variants of horns existed, such as chordal horns, fanfare horns, hunting horns, and glaurs. Horns were typically made of brass, bronze, horn or shell. The common horn, also known as a valveless trumpet, was traditional to kings courts.',
    'Hourglass' => 'Hourglass',
    'Hourglass description' => 'An hourglass consisted of two bulbous, glass globes connected by a narrow passage. These globes were held together by a frame, typically made of wood, that itself was held together by twine and screws. The glass globes were filled with sand that traveled from the topmost bulb to the lower one, passing through the narrow passage that connected them.',
    'Hunting trap' => 'Hunting trap',
    'Hunting trap description' => 'When you use your action to set it, this trap forms a saw-toothed steel ring that snaps shut when a creature steps on a pressure plate in the center. The trap is affixed by a heavy chain to an immobile object, such as a tree or a spike driven into the ground. A creature that steps on the plate must succeed on a DC 13 Dexterity saving throw or take 1d4 piercing damage and stop moving. Thereafter, until the creature breaks free of the trap, its movement is limited by the length of the chain (typically 3 feet long). A creature can use its action to make a DC 13 Strength check, freeing itself or another creature within its reach on a success. Each failed check deals 1 piercing damage to the trapped creature.',
    'Ink (1 ounce bottle)' => 'Ink (1 ounce bottle)',
    'Ink (1 ounce bottle) description' => 'Those who destroy knowledge, with ink, fire, or sword, are themselves destroyed. Ink was usually black, but was often colored to be red.',
    'Ink pen' => 'Ink pen',
    'Ink pen description' => 'A pen, also called an ink pen, was a writing utensil and drawing device.',
    'Insignia of rank' => 'Insignia of rank',
    'Insignia of rank description' => 'An insignia of rank is a visual symbol or emblem that denotes a persons position or status within a hierarchical organization, typically a military, governmental, or other structured group.',
    'Iron War Helmet' => 'Iron War Helmet',
    'Iron War Helmet description' => 'A battered iron helmet reinforced with riveted metal plates. Though heavy and somewhat restrictive, it offers excellent protection to the wearer\'s head. Its worn surface bears the scars of countless battles.',
    'Javelin' => 'Javelin',
    'Javelin description' => 'Javelins were light, flexible spears designed primarily as a throwing weapon, but were equally suitable for melee. They were usually constructed of wood and tapered to a sharp point at one or both ends. Alternatively, they could be tipped with a small metal spearhead. The shaft was much thinner than a spear intended for melee combat.',
    'Jewelers tools' => 'Jewelers tools',
    'Jewelers tools description' => null,
    'Jug or pitcher' => 'Jug or pitcher',
    'Jug or pitcher description' => 'A typical jug held 1 gal (3.8 L) of liquid, such as water or milk. They were usually made from ceramic clay (sometimes earthenware), glass, or metals, formed with a narrow neck and given a stopper to seal it shut. Some jugs had spouts or lips designed to effect accurate pouring, and a handle to make them easier to carry. Pitchers were usually open-topped and designed specifically for pouring, with a molded spout and sturdy handle. Decanters resembled large, decorative bottles, intended for pouring fine drinks such as wine or sparkling water. They would have a handle to ensure that body heat from holding it did not warm the beverage within.',
    'Ladder (10-foot)' => 'Ladder (10-foot)',
    'Ladder (10-foot) description' => 'A ladder was basically two parallel wooden poles connected by a series of evenly-spaced rungs. It was used by leaning it against a wall, and once in place it allowed the user to reach the higher vantage point. A ladder could also be set horizontally across a gap, creating a simple bridge, though they did not bear the weight as easily then. Exceptionally large ladders used for scaling castle walls were known as siege ladders. Though a simple and direct solution, siege ladders were highly dependent on the defenders being taken by surprise or otherwise distracted, often in conjunction with other siege engines.',
    'Lamp' => 'Lamp',
    'Lamp description' => 'A lamp casts bright light in a 15-foot radius and dim light for an additional 30 feet. Once lit, il burns for 6 hours on a flask (1 pint) of oil.',
    'Lance' => 'Lance',
    'Lance description' => 'The design of these spears varied between cultures, but they generally consisted of a long shaft made of tough wood with an iron head shaped like a laurel. These spearheads had cutting edges and a sharp point meant to penetrate armor. Also fitted to the shaft, just behind where a wielder would grip the lance, was the "graper" — a thick leather ring that acted as a stop against the wielders armpit. This was meant to halt any rearward momentum brought about by impacts.',
    'Lantern, bullseye' => 'Lantern, bullseye',
    'Lantern, bullseye description' => 'A bullseye lantern casts bright light in a 60-foot cone and dim light for an additional 60 feet. Once lit, it burns for 6 hours on a flask (1 pint) of oil.',
    'Lantern, hooded' => 'Lantern, hooded',
    'Lantern, hooded description' => 'A hooded lantern casts bright light in a 30-foot radius and dim light for an additional 30 feet. Once lit, it burns for 6 hours on a flask (1 pint) of oil. As an action, you can lower the hood, reducing the light lo dim light in a 5-foot radius.',
    'Le parchemin du Chevalier Noir' => 'Le parchemin du Chevalier Noir',
    'Le parchemin du Chevalier Noir description' => 'Un parchemin caché dans un tableau',
    'Leather' => 'Leather',
    'Leather description' => 'The breastplate and shoulder protectors of this armor are made of leather that has been stiffened by being boiled in oil. The rest of the armor is made of softer and more flexible materials',
    'Leatherworkers tools' => 'Leatherworkers tools',
    'Leatherworkers tools description' => null,
    'Letter' => 'Letter',
    'Letter description' => 'A letter from a dead colleague posing a question you have not yet been able to answer',
    'Light hammer' => 'Light hammer',
    'Light hammer description' => 'The light hammer is a small sledge hammer, usually with a haft made of wood about one and a half feet long. Later versions of this weapon added a pick on the back of the hammer head. An average light hammer costs 1gp and weighs 2lbs (0.9kg).',
    'Lock' => 'Lock',
    'Lock description' => 'A key is provided with the lock. Without the key, a creature proficient with thieves tools can pick this lock with a successful DC 15 Dexterity check. Your DM may decide that better locks are available for higher prices.',
    'Longbow' => 'Longbow',
    'Longbow description' => 'The longbow is a flexible shaft of wood (or horn or bone) about 4-6 ft (1.2-1.8 m) long with the ends connected by a bowstring that was a little shorter than the shaft, causing the wood to bend and keep the string under tension. For a composite longbow, the shaft is made from laminated materials (again, horn, wood or bone) and fashioned with a recurve, meaning the bow remains bow-shaped even when unstrung. It was commonly known that it took about five years of training to become proficient at the use of the longbow.',
    'Longsword' => 'Longsword',
    'Longsword description' => 'The design and length of longswords varied between cultures, they could even vary within the same culture depending upon the era. However, the length of longswords generally ranged between 35? to ?47 inches (0.89? to ?1.2 meters). Most longswords had a double-edged blade, though some had a single-edged blade, with a sharp point at their tip. They often had grooves known as "fullers" that ran the length of their blade, one on each surface, that made the blade both lighter and more flexible.',
    'Lute' => 'Lute',
    'Lute description' => 'A lute was a stringed instrument with a neck and a wooden soundboard, which was often ovoid or shaped as a teardrop. Lutes were often used by bards of the Realms. Lutes typically had a round back, so they rested easily on the players stomach. Doubled strings could be used to emit a fuller sound, and these were easily tuned by pegs.',
    'Lyre' => 'Lyre',
    'Lyre description' => 'A lyre was an ancient and portable stringed musical instrument. Lyres provided good accompaniment and background music for poems and epic recitals through the ages.',
    'Mace' => 'Mace',
    'Mace description' => 'The mace is a common variant of the mace family of weapons. Its a simple, one-handed melee weapon. A simple but effective mace, mass-cast by human blacksmiths.',
    'Magnifying glass' => 'Magnifying glass',
    'Magnifying glass description' => 'This lens allows a closer look at small objects. It is also useful as a substitute for flint and steel when starting tires. Lighting a fire with a magnifying glass requires light as bright as sunlight to focus, tinder to ignite, and about 5 minutes for the fire to ignite. A magnifying glass grants advantage on any ability check made to appraise or inspect an item that is small or highly detailed.',
    'Malice' => 'Malice',
    'Malice description' => 'A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution saving throw or become poisoned for 1 hour. The poisoned creature is blinded.',
    'Manacles' => 'Manacles',
    'Manacles description' => 'These metal restraints can bind a Small or Medium creature. Escaping the manacles requires a successful DC 20 Dexterity check. Breaking them requires a successful DC 20 Strength check. Each set of manacles comes with one key. Without the key, a creature proficient with thieves tools can pick the manacles lock with a successful DC 15 Dexterity check. Manacles have 15 hit points.',
    'Masons tools' => 'Masons tools',
    'Masons tools description' => 'These sets of tools included hammers, chisels, trowels, brushes, and squares. Those proficient with these tools were skilled at inspecting stonework and even spotting weak points in walls made of stone. This made them able to take down stone structures with ease. They were also able to determine the approximate date that stone buildings were constructed, as well as the potential builders. Dwarves were often proficient with these tools.',
    'Maul' => 'Maul',
    'Maul description' => 'Maul is a common variant of the Mauls family of weapons. Hammers like this are used in many dwarven competitions, testing who can throw them the farthest while enduring the most ale.',
    'Mess kit' => 'Mess kit',
    'Mess kit description' => 'This tin box contains a cup and simple cutlery. The box clamps together, and one side can be used as a cooking pan and the other as a plate or shallow bowl.',
    'Midnight tears' => 'Midnight tears',
    'Midnight tears description' => 'A creature that ingests this poison suffers no effect until the stroke of midnight. If the poison has not been neutralized before then, the creature must succeed on a DC 17 Constitution saving throw, taking 31 (9d6) poison damage on a failed save, or half as much damage on a successful one.',
    'Mirror, steel' => 'Mirror, steel',
    'Mirror, steel description' => 'Typical hand mirrors carried by adventurers were usually made of polished steel. Small mirrors with handles were essential components of thieves tools.',
    'Morningstar' => 'Morningstar',
    'Morningstar description' => 'Morningstar is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Morningstars family of weapons. It is a martial melee weapon wielded in one hand. More of this morningstars iron spikes are bent than not. A tuft of hair is caked to the base of one of this morningstars jagged spiked.',
    'Navigators tools' => 'Navigators tools',
    'Navigators tools description' => 'This set of instruments is used for navigation at sea. Proficiency with navigators tools lets you chart a ships course and follow navigation charts. In addition, these tools allow you to add your proficiency bonus to any ability check you make to avoid getting lost at sea.',
    'Net' => 'Net',
    'Net description' => 'Net is a Class Action available to Kuo-toa Hunters and Sahuagin Hunters. This ability allows the user to throw a Net at their targets, possibly ensnaring them.',
    'Oil (flask)' => 'Oil (flask)',
    'Oil (flask) description' => 'Oil usually comes in a clay flask that holds 1 pint. As an action, you can splash the oil in this flask onto a creature within 5 feet of you or throw it up to 20 feet, shattering it on impact. Make a ranged attack against a target creature or object, treating the oil as an improvised weapon. On a hit, the target is covered in oil. If the target takes any fire damage before the oil dries (after 1 minute), the target takes an additional 5 fire damage from the burning oil. You can also pour a flask of oil on the ground to cover a 5-foot-square area, provided that the surface is level. If lit, the oil burns for 2 rounds and deals 5 fire damage to any creature that enters the area or ends its turn in the area. A creature can take this damage only once per turn.',
    'Oil of taggit' => 'Oil of taggit',
    'Oil of taggit description' => 'A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 13 Constitution saving throw or become poisoned for 24 hours. The poisoned creature is unconscious. The creature wakes up if it takes damage.',
    'Old rusted iron key' => 'Old rusted iron key',
    'Old rusted iron key description' => 'A rusty wrought iron key, 12.5 cm long, with a twisted oval ring and 5 asymmetrical teeth with serrated edges. The rust is reddish-brown with blackish areas and chips revealing a bluish-gray metal.
Partially erased engravings depict a Latin cross and crescent moons. Traces of cobwebs and white dust adhere to the crevices.
The key is lukewarm, with greenish reflections under UV light, and casts a distorted shadow in the shape of a skeletal hand.',
    'Orb' => 'Orb',
    'Orb description' => 'Orbs all shared their spherical shape, though many were constructed from different materials or were different colors.',
    'Padded' => 'Padded',
    'Padded description' => 'Padded armor consists of quilted layers of cloth and batting',
    'Painters supplies' => 'Painters supplies',
    'Painters supplies description' => null,
    'Pale tincture' => 'Pale tincture',
    'Pale tincture description' => 'A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 16 Constitution saving throw or take 3 (1d6) poison damage and become poisoned. The poisoned creature must repeat the saving throw every 24 hours, taking 3 (1d6) poison damage on a failed save. Until this poison ends, the damage the poison deals can’t be healed by any means. After seven successful saving throws, the effect ends and the creature can heal normally.',
    'Pan flute' => 'Pan flute',
    'Pan flute description' => 'Birdpipes, also known as a shalm, satyr pipes, panpipes, pan flute, or syrinx, were a variety of flute instrument. Birdpipes were somewhat related to the common longhorn. They consisted of a series of hollow tubes of different lengths, all vertical, that were fastened together in a row from smallest to largest. This instrument was played by blowing across the tops of its pipes, as well as moving them from side to side to play different notes. They produced a sweet, rippling, rich sound that was said to be capable of charming ferocious animals.',
    'Paper (one sheet)' => 'Paper (one sheet)',
    'Paper (one sheet) description' => 'Paper was a thin sheet of pressed wood pulp or cloth fibers, and was a popular writing surface sold by the sheet or in long rolls. That said, it was not the only writing surface available in the Realms, as there existed vellum and parchment derived from the hides of animals. Other surfaces included paper made from bamboo, papyrus, rice, and pieces of flat slate stone.',
    'Parchment (one sheet)' => 'Parchment (one sheet)',
    'Parchment (one sheet) description' => null,
    'Perfume (vial)' => 'Perfume (vial)',
    'Perfume (vial) description' => 'Perfume was stored in vials and bottles that were sometimes covered with sealing wax. Scents were made from a wide variety of natural and processed materials, such as vegetable oils, plant sap and resin, herbs, spices, animal fluids and organs, and minerals.',
    'Pick, miners' => 'Pick, miners',
    'Pick, miners description' => null,
    'Pike' => 'Pike',
    'Pike description' => 'Pike is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Pikes family of weapons. It is a martial melee weapon thats wielded with both hands. It gives the user extra reach in melee combat. This pikes broad steel head has been repeatedly bent and hammered back into its original shape.',
    'Piton' => 'Piton',
    'Piton description' => 'A piton, also called a spike, was a tool that could be wedged into a crevice of a rock in order to support a climber. Pitons were specialized iron or steel spikes with an eyehole for attaching a rope, carabiner, or other device.',
    'Plate' => 'Plate',
    'Plate description' => 'Plate consists of shaped, interlocking metal plates to cover the entire body. A suit of plate includes gauntlets, heavy leather boots, a visored helmet, and thick layers of padding underneath the armor. Buckles and straps distribute the weight over the body',
    'Playing card set' => 'Playing card set',
    'Playing card set description' => null,
    'Poison, basic (vial)' => 'Poison, basic (vial)',
    'Poison, basic (vial) description' => 'You can use the poison in this vial to coat one slashing or piercing weapon or up to three pieces of ammunition. Applying the poison takes an action. A creature hit by the poisoned weapon or ammunition must make a DC 10 Constitution saving throw or take 1d4 poison damage. Once applied, the poison retains potency for 1 minute before drying.',
    'Poisoners kit' => 'Poisoners kit',
    'Poisoners kit description' => 'A poisoners kit includes the vials, chemicals, and other equipment necessary for the creation of poisons. Proficiency with this kit lets you add your proficiency bonus to any ability checks you make to craft or use poisons.',
    'Pole (10-foot)' => 'Pole (10-foot)',
    'Pole (10-foot) description' => 'As befitting their name, 10-foot poles were often 10 feet (3 meters) long. While this made them useful, they were also quite cumbersome and bulky, especially in tight spaces such as dungeons.',
    'Pot, iron' => 'Pot, iron',
    'Pot, iron description' => 'A pot was a deep dish used for cooking food, such as boiling or roasting, and often had two handles and a matching lid. Firegrates were handy and simple devices that allowed a camper to put a pot over a small campfire.',
    'Potion of healing' => 'Potion of healing',
    'Potion of healing description' => 'A character who drinks the magical red fluid in this vial regains 2d4 + 2 hit points. Drinking or administering a potion takes an action.',
    'Potters tools' => 'Potters tools',
    'Potters tools description' => null,
    'Pouch' => 'Pouch',
    'Pouch description' => 'A cloth or leather pouch can hold up to 20 sling bullets or 50 blowgun needles, among other things. A compartmentalized pouch for holding spell components is called a component pouch (described earlier in this section).',
    'Prayer book' => 'Prayer book',
    'Prayer book description' => 'The prayer book is essentially a collection of religious texts, prayers, hymns, and rituals associated with the characters deity or faith. Alternatively, it could be a prayer wheel, which serves a similar purpose, especially in some faiths. Its primarily allowing the character to express their devotion and connection to their faith. It can be used during religious ceremonies, quiet moments of reflection, or even in times of stress. It can provide a source of comfort and guidance for the character.',
    'Priests Pack' => 'Priests Pack',
    'Priests Pack description' => 'Includes a backpack, a blanket, 10 candles, a tinderbox, an alms box, 2 blocks of incense, a censer, vestments, 2 days of rations, and a waterskin.',
    'Purple worm poison' => 'Purple worm poison',
    'Purple worm poison description' => 'This poison must be harvested from a dead or incapacitated purple worm. A creature subjected to this poison must make a DC 19 Constitution saving throw, taking 42 (12d6) poison damage on a failed save, or half as much damage on a successful one.',
    'Purse' => 'Purse',
    'Purse description' => 'A purse is a small bag or pouch, typically used for carrying personal items such as money, keys, identification, and other small valuables. Its primary function is to hold and organize small, essential items for easy access. It serves as a portable storage solution for personal belongings.',
    'Quarterstaff' => 'Quarterstaff',
    'Quarterstaff description' => 'Quarterstaff is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Quarterstaves family of weapons. It is a simple melee weapon that can be wielded in one hand, or with both hands for extra damage. Someone has carved twenty-seven small lines into the hard wood.',
    'Quill' => 'Quill',
    'Quill description' => 'A quill is a pen made from a birds feather, used for writing with ink. Its a common item found in most settings.',
    'Quiver' => 'Quiver',
    'Quiver description' => 'A quiver can hold up to 20 arrows.',
    'Ram, portable' => 'Ram, portable',
    'Ram, portable description' => 'You can use a portable ram to break down doors. When doing so, you gain a +4 bonus on the Strength check. One other character can help you use the ram, giving you advantage on this check.',
    'Rapier' => 'Rapier',
    'Rapier description' => 'Rapier is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Rapiers family of weapons. It is a martial melee weapon wielded in one hand. As a finesse weapon, it can benefit from the wielders Dexterity and not just their Strength. A slender but deadly sword, favoured by Waterdhavian nobility.',
    'Rations (1 day)' => 'Rations (1 day)',
    'Rations (1 day) description' => 'Rations consist of dry foods suitable for extended travel, including jerky, dried fruit, hardtack, and nuts.',
    'Reliquary' => 'Reliquary',
    'Reliquary description' => null,
    'Ring Mail' => 'Ring Mail',
    'Ring Mail description' => 'This armor is leather armor with heavy rings sewn into it. The rings he1p reinforce the armor against blows from swords and axes. Ring mail is inferior to chain mail, and it is usually worn only by those who cant afford better armor',
    'Robes' => 'Robes',
    'Robes description' => 'A robe was a long garment that was usually made of cloth. Many spellcasters and scholars, such as wizards, wore robes that were enchanted with magical powers. Robes usually started at shoulder height, and ended close to the ankles. New, standard robes cost around 1 gp, and weighed 4 pounds (1.8 kilograms). Extremely rare and enchanted robes could cost several tens of thousands of gold pieces.',
    'Rod' => 'Rod',
    'Rod description' => 'Rods were scepter-like objects that could be used by spellcasters to focus their spells. They were particularly favored by warlocks. Some magical rods had unique magical powers.',
    'Rope, hempen (50 feet)' => 'Rope, hempen (50 feet)',
    'Rope, hempen (50 feet) description' => 'Rope made from hemp is durable and rugged. The diameter of hemp rope varied, from as small as 0.25 inches (0.64 centimeters) to more than 3 inches (7.6 centimeters). Hemp rope with large diameters was commonly used aboard ships. Hemp rope was heavy. A 50 feet (15 meters) length of hemp rope could weight up to 20 pounds (9.1 kilograms). However, a 50 feet (15 meters) length of hemp rope could hold over 500 pounds (230 kilograms) in weight.',
    'Rope, silk (50 feet)' => 'Rope, silk (50 feet)',
    'Rope, silk (50 feet) description' => 'Rope made from silk was not as strong as hemp rope but was much lighter. Silk rope was made from long threads of silk, which were braided together into cords, and multiple cord braided together to make the rope. It had a smoother texture than hemp rope. A 50 feet (15 meters) length of silk rope weighed only 8 pounds (3.6 kilograms) but could only hold up to 500 pounds (230 kilograms) in weight. It was also much more expensive than hemp rope.',
    'Sack' => 'Sack',
    'Sack description' => null,
    'Scale Mail' => 'Scale Mail',
    'Scale Mail description' => 'This armor consists of a coat and leggings (and perhaps a separate skirt) of leather covered with overlapping pieces of metal, much like the scales of a fish. The suit includes gauntlets',
    'Scale, merchants' => 'Scale, merchants',
    'Scale, merchants description' => 'A scale includes a small balance, pans, and a suitable assortment of weights up to 2 pounds. With it, you can measure the exact weight of small objects, such as raw precious metals or trade goods, to help determine their worth.',
    'Scholars Pack' => 'Scholars Pack',
    'Scholars Pack description' => 'Includes a backpack, a book of lore, a bottle of ink, an ink pen, 10 sheets of parchment, a little bag of sand, and a small knife.',
    'Scimitar' => 'Scimitar',
    'Scimitar description' => 'Scimitar is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Scimitars family of weapons. It is a martial melee weapon wielded in one hand. As a finesse weapon, it can benefit from the wielders Dexterity and not just their Strength. Its a light weapon that anyone can dual-wield without special training. This human-made scimitar falls far short of replicating the quality of the drow style it clearly imitates.',
    'Scroll of pedigree' => 'Scroll of pedigree',
    'Scroll of pedigree description' => 'A scroll of pedigree is a document that verifies a characters noble lineage. It essentially serves as proof of their ancestry and their connection to a noble family. It often contains a detailed family tree, coats of arms, and other information that establishes the characters noble heritage.',
    'Sealing wax' => 'Sealing wax',
    'Sealing wax description' => 'Sealing wax was a type of wax used to make seals and insignia. As this adhesive wax was used to seal both letters and legal contracts, it was often carried by diplomats and forgers.',
    'Serpent venom' => 'Serpent venom',
    'Serpent venom description' => 'This poison must be harvested from a dead or incapacitated giant poisonous snake. A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 11 Constitution saving throw, taking 10 (3d6) poison damage on a failed save, or half as much damage on a successful one.',
    'Shawm' => 'Shawm',
    'Shawm description' => 'A shawm or shaum was a double-reed instrument similar to an oboe. Its sound was loud, shrill and buzzing. If played well, the sound of a shawm was clear and melancholic, reminiscent of migrating geese.',
    'Shield' => 'Shield',
    'Shield description' => 'Shields were usually made from wood or metal, and were used by combatants to gain extra protection. Shields were carried in one hand, usually strapped to the arm that carried them. Smaller shields could be used without compromising the use of both hands. Shields could be used in attacks to bash or push opponents, and some shields had spikes attached to them be more devastating when used as a weapon.',
    'Shortbow' => 'Shortbow',
    'Shortbow description' => 'Shortbow is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Shortbows family of weapons. It is a simple ranged weapon thats wielded with both hands. This small, agile bow bears a beautiful elven string, easy to draw but tough as steel.',
    'Shortsword' => 'Shortsword',
    'Shortsword description' => 'Shortsword is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Shortswords family of weapons. It is a martial melee weapon wielded in one hand. As a finesse weapon, it can benefit from the wielders Dexterity and not just their Strength. Its a light weapon that anyone can dual-wield without special training. A common and effective sword, carried by adventurers, thieves, and watchmen alike.',
    'Shovel' => 'Shovel',
    'Shovel description' => null,
    'Sickle' => 'Sickle',
    'Sickle description' => 'Sickle is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Sickles family of weapons. It is a simple melee weapon wielded in one hand. Its a light weapon that anyone can dual-wield without special training. A farming tool with a wicked edge, spilling blood and grain with equal ease.',
    'Signal whistle' => 'Signal whistle',
    'Signal whistle description' => null,
    'Signet ring' => 'Signet ring',
    'Signet ring description' => null,
    'Sling' => 'Sling',
    'Sling description' => 'Sling is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Slings family of weapons. It is a simple ranged weapon wielded in one hand. Big, neat stitches line the supple leather pouch of this sling',
    'Sling bullets' => 'Sling bullets',
    'Sling bullets description' => 'Bullets were spherically shaped and usually pieces of lead, which was very dense. Sling bullets could also be made of copper or even exotic materials, such as the knuckles of salamanders or chips of elementals. They most often came in leather pouches, which could hold ten of them. Like arrows and bolts, some bullets were enchanted in some way so that they could deal more damage. Bullets that struck targets were destroyed in the process, but had a chance of being recovered.',
    'Smiths tools' => 'Smiths tools',
    'Smiths tools description' => null,
    'Soap' => 'Soap',
    'Soap description' => 'Soap was a cleaning substance created and used throughout the realms, even though was sometimes hard to find. When lathered it created foam that removed dirt and smell from skin, clothing, adventuring gear, or hair.',
    'Spear' => 'Spear',
    'Spear description' => 'Spear is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Spears family of weapons. It is a simple melee weapon that can be wielded in one hand, or with both hands for extra damage. Its design lends itself well to be thrown at enemies as a projectile. Its design doesnt lend itself to be dual-wielded, even by those with special training in dual-wielding. An aged spear, its steel head nicked by the blows of a dozen battles.',
    'Spell book' => 'Spell book',
    'Spell book description' => 'Essential for wizards, a spellbook is a leather-bound tome with 100 blank vellum pages suitable for recording spells.',
    'Spikes, iron' => 'Spikes, iron',
    'Spikes, iron description' => 'Spikes were most often between 6-inch (15-centimeter) and 12-inch (30-centimeter) long, made from iron with a square cross-section. Depending on their purpose they could be made with a simple flattened head, or with an eyehole for looping a rope, carabiner, or some other device. Aside from bolts, nails, and pegs, spikes also had a resemblance to stakes.',
    'Splint' => 'Splint',
    'Splint description' => 'This armor is made of narrow vertical strips of metal riveted to a backing of leather that is worn over cloth padding. Flexible chain mail protects the joints',
    'Sprig of mistletoe' => 'Sprig of mistletoe',
    'Sprig of mistletoe description' => 'Mistletoe was a type of small plant in the Realms.',
    'Spyglass' => 'Spyglass',
    'Spyglass description' => 'Objects viewed through a spyglass are magnified to twice their size.',
    'Staff' => 'Staff',
    'Staff description' => null,
    'Sticks of incense' => 'Sticks of incense',
    'Sticks of incense description' => 'Incense is often associated with religious ceremonies, rituals, and meditation. It adds an atmospheric element to these activities. It can be used to create a sense of sacredness or to enhance spiritual focus. Certain spells require incense as a material component. This means that the caster must have incense on hand to cast the spell.',
    'Studded Leather' => 'Studded Leather',
    'Studded Leather description' => 'Made from tough but flexible leather, studded leather is reinforced with close-set rivets or spikes',
    'Tent, two-person' => 'Tent, two-person',
    'Tent, two-person description' => 'A simple and portable canvas shelter, a tent sleeps two.',
    'Thieves tools' => 'Thieves tools',
    'Thieves tools description' => 'This set of tools includes a small file, a set of lock picks, a small mirror mounted on a metal handle, a set of narrow-bladed scissors, and a pair of pliers. Proficiency with these tools lets you add your proficiency bonus to any ability checks you make to disarm traps or open locks.',
    'Three-Dragon Ante set' => 'Three-Dragon Ante set',
    'Three-Dragon Ante set description' => 'Three-Dragon Ante was a card game played in many taverns across Faerûn, including Waterdeep and Baldurs Gate in the late 15th century DR. Played across all levels of society, the game was sometimes called copper dragons or three-copper when played by peasants, as well as golds or plats when played by adventurers and aristocrats.',
    'Tinderbox' => 'Tinderbox',
    'Tinderbox description' => 'This small container holds flint, fire steel, and tinder (usually dry cloth soaked in light oil) used lo kindle a fire. Using it to light a torch - or anything else with abundant, exposed fuel - takes an action. Lighting any other fire takes 1 minute.',
    'Tinkers tools' => 'Tinkers tools',
    'Tinkers tools description' => null,
    'Torch' => 'Torch',
    'Torch description' => 'A torch burns for 1 hour, providing bright light in a 20-foot radius and dim light for an additional 20 feet. If you make a melee attack with a burning torch and hit, it deals 1 fire damage.',
    'Torpor' => 'Torpor',
    'Torpor description' => 'A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution saving throw or become poisoned for 4d6 hours. The poisoned creature is incapacitated.',
    'Totem' => 'Totem',
    'Totem description' => 'A totem was a symbolic representation of a spirit. They were typically used in barbarian tribes and beast cults. The practice was most common in the North, and they were often considered just as sacred as what they represented. Tribes generally had only one totem spirit that they worshiped, though there were exceptions to this.',
    'Trident' => 'Trident',
    'Trident description' => 'Trident is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Tridents family of weapons. It is a martial melee weapon that can be wielded in one hand, or with both hands for extra damage. Its design lends itself well to be thrown at enemies as a projectile. It is said tridents were brought to the surface when Umberlee threw a Triton prince who scorned her onto Faerûns shores.',
    'Truth serum' => 'Truth serum',
    'Truth serum description' => 'A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 11 Constitution saving throw or become poisoned for 1 hour. The poisoned creature can’t knowingly speak a lie, as if under the effect of a zone of truth spell.',
    'Unarmed strike' => 'Unarmed strike',
    'Unarmed strike description' => 'Unarmed Strike is a common Action that allows the user to make an attack with their bare fists when no melee weapons are equipped.',
    'Vestments' => 'Vestments',
    'Vestments description' => 'Vestments serve to signify the wearers role and status within their faith. They add a sense of formality and sacredness to religious ceremonies and rituals. They contribute to the overall atmosphere and immersion of the game world. Vestments can vary significantly in design and style, depending on the specific deity or religion. They may include robes, stoles, tunics, and other garments, often adorned with symbolic colors and patterns. The quality and elaborateness of vestments can also reflect the wealth and importance of the religious institution.',
    'Vial' => 'Vial',
    'Vial description' => 'Vials were small glass containers that were used to hold liquids and powders. They could be used in laboratories to conduct experiments or to transport these substances.',
    'Viol' => 'Viol',
    'Viol description' => 'A viol was a six-string instrument similar to a violin. Viols had a deeply resonant sound that conveyed melancholy themes very well. They could be played on their own or as part of larger ensembles. Viols were particularly favored by dwarves, owing to their deep and dark sounds',
    'Wand' => 'Wand',
    'Wand description' => 'Wands were spellcasting implements commonly used by artificers, bards, warlocks, and wizards alike. Usually constructed from wood, many wands typically had arcane energies of their own. Sometimes this energy allowed the caster to simply empower their spells, while other wands possessed the ability to cast their own.',
    'War pick' => 'War pick',
    'War pick description' => 'War Pick is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the War Picks family of weapons. It is a martial melee weapon wielded in one hand. A pickaxe modified to pierce flesh and armour rather than stone.',
    'Warhammer' => 'Warhammer',
    'Warhammer description' => 'Warhammer is a mundane, nonmagical variant of the Warhammers family of weapons. It is a martial melee weapon that can be wielded in one hand, or with both hands for extra damage. This hammers noticeable lack of upkeep hardly matters, given its heft.',
    'Waterskin' => 'Waterskin',
    'Waterskin description' => 'Waterskins were often shaped like kidneys and made of goatskin, but they could also come in a variety of other styles.',
